Bell's Palsy
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Unilateral facial paralysis, Inability to wrinkle forehead, Hyperacusis/Altered taste

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-003
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of facial droop, raising patient fears of an acute stroke.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden unilateral facial paralysis and slurred speech. Urgent face-to-face assessment is required to clinically differentiate a peripheral facial nerve palsy from an acute cerebrovascular accident (stroke) within the thrombolysis window, preventing devastating morbidity.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up and the right side of my face was completely frozen. I can't even blink my eye or hold water in my mouth."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an acute, sudden onset of unilateral facial muscle weakness that rapidly progressed over the last 12 hours. Symptoms were preceded by a deep post-auricular pain and aching yesterday evening. The patient describes an inability to close the ipsilateral eye tightly, resulting in lagophthalmos, and drooling from the affected side of the mouth during drinking. There is noticeable ipsilateral hyperacusis resulting from paralysis of the stapedius muscle, and an altered taste sensation specifically on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. No limb weakness, visual loss, or true dysphasia.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but currently distressed and struggling with oral intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of corneal abrasion due to impaired eye closure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, dabbing right side of mouth with a tissue.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals unilateral facial paralysis on the right. There is complete loss of forehead wrinkling on the affected side indicating lower motor neuron involvement. Asymmetrical smile with drooping of the oral commissure and flattening of the ipsilateral nasolabial fold. Inability to wrinkle the forehead is explicitly noted. Altered taste and hyperacusis are confirmed. Upper and lower extremity power, sensation, and coordination are entirely normal. No ataxia. Speech is slightly slurred due to lip weakness, but language is intact.
Environmental Observations: Safe, well-lit environment. No mobility issues.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Nil known drug allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bell's Palsy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete paralysis of the upper face (forehead), absence of hemiparesis, and no speech/language deficits.

Herpes Zoster Oticus (Ramsay Hunt Syndrome) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of vesicular eruption in the external auditory canal or oropharynx.

Parotid Gland Tumor - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden onset and absence of a palpable mass in the parotid region.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, frightening onset of unilateral facial paralysis. Telephone triage appropriately flagged this as a potential cerebrovascular accident. Immediate clinical assessment was imperative to definitively distinguish a benign peripheral neuropathy (Bell's Palsy) from a central neurological emergency requiring time-critical thrombolysis. Deferring examination until the morning would have caused immense psychological distress and missed the critical therapeutic window if the etiology was ischemic. The examination definitively localized the lesion to the peripheral facial nerve, allowing safe outpatient management and initiation of early corticosteroids to optimize functional recovery.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Prednisolone 50mg daily for 10 days. Advised lubricating eye drops hourly during the day and ophthalmic ointment with an eye patch at night.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By applying lubricating drops every hour, the patient actively prevents corneal drying, ensuring the eye remains pain-free and protected.
Step 2: Taping the eye shut at night provides physical barrier protection, granting the patient uninterrupted sleep without fear of eye injury.
Step 3: Chewing food carefully on the unaffected side immediately reduces drooling and prevents pocketing, leading to a cleaner and more comfortable dining experience.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Eye patch, medical tape.
Follow-up: Review with regular GP in 1 week to assess recovery progress.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop any weakness or numbness in the arms or legs, difficulty finding words, double vision, or a severe, sudden-onset headache.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Maximum weakness typically occurs within 48-72 hours. Gradual improvement is expected over weeks to months. Steroids initiated early improve the chance of complete recovery.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent review if you develop a painful, blistering rash around the ear or face, or if the eye becomes profoundly red, painful, or develops a discharge indicating corneal ulceration.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Prednisolone reduces inflammation and swelling around the facial nerve, improving blood flow and facilitating recovery. Eye care is critical because the paralyzed eyelid cannot naturally protect the cornea.
Actionable Steps: Start the steroid medication immediately. Tape the eye closed tonight with the ointment. Eat slowly on the unaffected side.

